## Exploit Detail Review

### 49742.py - OpenEMR 4.1.0 'u' SQL Injection

| Attribute | Detail |
|-----------|--------|
| CVE/Reference | Exploit-DB 49742 |
| Target endpoint | /openemr/interface/login/validateUser.php?u= |
| Authentication required | None - pre-authentication vulnerability |
| Injection type | Time-based blind SQL injection |
| Technique | Sleep-based character extraction via ASCII comparison |
| Impact | Extracts username and password hash pairs from users table |
| Correlation to enumeration | validateUser.php?u= confirmed present in login.php AJAX source during Phase 2 |␀

### 17998.txt - Openemr-4.1.0 SQL Injection

| Attribute | Detail |
|-----------|--------|
| CVE/Reference | Exploit-DB 17998, OSVDB-70134 |
| Target endpoint | /openemr/interface/patient_file/summary/add_edit_issue.php?issue= |␀
| Authentication required | Yes - requires active session |
| Injection type | UNION-based SQL injection |
| Impact | Database enumeration with authenticated access |
| Chain position | Secondary - only accessible post-authentication |

[7m## Attack Chain Rationale[27m

[7mA two-stage chain is proposed based on evidence:[27m

[7mStage 1 - Unauthenticated credential extraction:[27m
[7mExploit the time-based blind SQL injection in validateUser.php (49742.py) to extract[27m[7m [27m[7musername and password hash pairs from the OpenEMR users table. This requires no pri[27m[7mo[27m[7mr authentication and directly targets an endpoint confirmed present during Phase 2 [27m[7me[27m[7mnumeration.␀[27m

[7mStage 2 - Authenticated access and further exploitation:[27m
[7mUse extracted credentials (after hash cracking if required) to authenticate to OpenE[27m[7mM[27m[7mR. Authenticated access opens additional attack surface including the UNION-based S[27m[7mQ[27m[7mLi in 17998.txt and potentially authenticated file upload or RCE vulnerabilities al[27m[7ms[27m[7mo present in the 4.1.x range.␀[27m

[7mLeast destructive viable path selection: The SQLi chain reads from the database with[27m[7mo[27m[7mut modifying application state, making it the lowest-impact entry method available.[27m[7m [27m[7mNo destructive payloads are required to establish initial access.␀[27m

